h of $y=3^x$ has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1),$ grows rapidly for $x>0,$ and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x<0.$ \n\nWhen $x=1,$ we have $y=3^1 = 3.$ Therefore, another point on the graph is $(1,3).$ \n\nWe draw the graph through this point as follows:\n\n![](images/43/4367540979396fd0106204a0f71d9368a70ce3538d8e7239ac250e5fc45644f1.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110311 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1216" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110311 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x) = 3^x?$\n\nI. It passes through the point $(1,3).$\n\nII. $f(x)\\rightarrow -\\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty.$\n\nIII. The graph of $y=f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote $x=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110311 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "45473f44-206e-5eb6-a663-04d264e6a890" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110311 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=3^x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/83/837e535b83d91c67e7e5c592bd71e9b9ebe59c216fc75ba4f99de2ccec2546ae.png)\n\nNow, let's look at each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true because $f(1) = 3^1 = 3.$\n\n- Statement II is false, since $f(x)\\rightarrow 0$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The graph has a *horizontal* asymptote $y=0.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110312 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1217" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110312 #edb/ref 1071 "Plot the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}(2)^x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110312 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3661675e-e7da-5834-97cf-017e4e084275" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110312 #edb/ref 1074 "First we plot the graph of $y=2^{x}$:\n\n![](images/94/946d749b38d09eebd11eba2821be07e61c6551f17ab9cff0149cdbf600257796.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph vertically by a factor of $\\dfrac12.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $\\left(0,\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/bc/bc971bc35c4f54baba330aa074bf9618fb7a755593f5f9e713bcc90142