6110875 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110875 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110875 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110875 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110875 #edb/ref 1045 "Graphing Exponential Growth Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110875 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186111702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110875 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110875 #edb/ref 1050 600.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110875 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "33a94a7b-379f-5b7b-bcb4-80cdb16140fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110930 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186107471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110930 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166992" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110930 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110930 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{7}{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}?$\n\nI. Its range is $(\\frac{2}{7},\\infty ).$\n\nII. Its domain is $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).$\n\nIII. It passes through the point $(1,\\frac{3}{7}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110930 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "afce8b19-fb25-5544-97f9-8f9caa8b68b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110930 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=\\frac{2}{7}{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/52/5259fcd21c4fe03e4facb4e8a49860122e051b8427e4342a829f150aa3b30e6e.png)\n\nNow, let's look at each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, since the range of the function is $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is false, since the domain of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is true. $f(1)=\\frac{2}{7}{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{1}=\\frac{2}{7}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{3}{7}.$ Therefore, the point $(1,\\frac{3}{7})$ lies on the curve.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111100 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186108340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111100 #edb/ref 1069 "q-26906" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111100 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111100 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following represents the graph of $y=0.5({9}^{x})?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111100 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "384c08ea-9724-53fb-a1dd-8c076acd8ade" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111100 #edb/ref 1074 "First we plot the graph of $y={9}^{x}$:\n\n![](images/27/270006d7d2e7d7cab172ba586df796af8b78d9b00157f439bf65cc0e151ed13e.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph vertically by a factor of $0.5.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $(0,0.5),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/3d/3d0e0db5029b92622d28c478c6a8d3ee02dee21fe3fa1f82965838b22d07f543.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111702 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111702 #edb/ref 1061 516446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111702 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "af86e00c-f654-5321-9355-dbe234a657e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111702 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111703 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111703 #edb/ref 1061 516447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111703 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9c436809-f5cb-527a-907e-bd891aa0951c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111703 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111704 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111704 #edb/ref 1061 516450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111704 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "de707c0a-c932-5251-81f5-798ae37e792e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111704 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111705 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111705 #edb/ref 1061 516453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111705 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "82779645-e04b-59c6-a05e-c042063885a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111705 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111706 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111706 #edb/ref 1061 516454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111706 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4b8c5352-dd55-52e0-83dd-102169a1a723" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111706 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111707 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111707 #edb/ref 1061 516458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111707 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "254e2522-a3c6-57f8-9ac1-0e813acdc6d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111707 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111708 #edb/ref 1061 516459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111708 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fbd6d68b-40b3-5cb7-afa4-487f4a8e9f03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111708 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111825 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of $y=2^x.$ First, we create a table of values:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y = 2^x$ | $\\dfrac18$ | $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/e9/e987a8647092c836bde4bcea31dc075e80e9da36a67133fc2b9431b99c5e42ed.png)\n\nNote the following key points:\n\n1\\. The graph has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1).$\n\n2\\. The graph is increasing.\n\n3\\. For $x>0,$ the graph grows very rapidly as $x$ increases. Symbolically, we write $y\\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow \\infty.$\n\n4\\. For $x<0,$ the graph gets very close to zero as $x$ decreases. However, it never actually reaches zero, so the graph has the horizontal asymptote $y=0.$ Symbolically, we write $y\\rightarrow 0$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty.$\n\n5\\. The domain is $x\\in (-\\infty , \\infty )$, and the range is $y>0.$\n\nThese key points apply to *any* exponential curve $y=a^x$ with $a>1.$ These graphs always have the same general shape:\n\n![](images/48/48f7bc7bb25bf1955d1e2cf48cffbf09e17f80a1228cada1c31f7ad4822bdace.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111825 #edb/ref 1065 1540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111825 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1a4d886d-d7b8-5667-b52a-2c2eb4a35b91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111825 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111826 #edb/ref 1064 "Multiplying an exponential growth function by a positive number **scales** the graph in the vertical direction.\n\nFor example, the curve ![](images/68/68dcb337cb0927d43403eebeb21c0a54dc46a136f7cd21ce821d9bfc325c748e.svg) represents a vertical scaling of ![](images/76/763bdfd3547d86e0c619eb55c57d6d3b83c3405c6fa04c01f4405a056475e9cf.svg) by a factor of ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg)  It takes all of the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates on ![](images/76/763bdfd3547d86e0c619eb55c57d6d3b83c3405c6fa04c01f4405a056475e9cf.svg) and multiplies them by ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg) This has the effect of **stretching** the curve upwards.\n\n![](images/70/7052037576cfaf29807f0ad332100b9929a72806846219da2599c8f911500d87.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote at ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) remained the same, and the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept moved from ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to ![](images/43/437619dfcf8db5be350de504dc9d98eab13c2ac21c62998d3c268a1f475a30d9.svg)\n\nIn general, for *any* vertically stretched exponential curve ![](images/8c/8ccfcf186ac190ccb8f91676958e6030b4224bfd46b3598261736fbfcb7c351f.svg)\n\n- the horizontal asymptote at ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) remains the same, and\n\n- the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept moves from ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to ![](images/e8/e8c4c8598a8228afb25cbf13c034e2fe234393f9b9c0dfde8be1726b4fc79a8e.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111826 #edb/ref 1065 1541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111826 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8c1fa510-e5f3-585b-852a-c46724144ec1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111826 #edb/ref 1067 "Vertical Stretching" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111833 #edb/ref 1064 "The curve ![](images/e9/e910a01694746b83e1662e73779d7e758399e2d0bdf7441bbd8221a37f278b17.svg) represents a vertical scaling of ![](images/76/763bdfd3547d86e0c619eb55c57d6d3b83c3405c6fa04c01f4405a056475e9cf.svg) by a factor of ![](images/79/79cadb0dadf367d4c7456e91f5847fa57d3f90d5129f383ca6c073782ecc6892.svg) It takes all of the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates on ![](images/76/763bdfd3547d86e0c619eb55c57d6d3b83c3405c6fa04c01f4405a056475e9cf.svg) and multiplies them by ![](images/79/79cadb0dadf367d4c7456e91f5847fa57d3f90d5129f383ca6c073782ecc6892.svg) This has the effect of squashing, or **compressing**, the curve downwards.\n\n![](images/68/685e360ed7bcd7975aa705d192ef487af17688bb113b20e10f881a0bdf7f46b7.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote at ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) remained the same, and the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept moved from ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to ![](images/97/97a51f546ed69cb656e8a811f3a4acf3f6dacce8e9ed7cac63419ad63f0497d1.svg)\n\nIn general for *any* vertically stretched exponential curve ![](images/8c/8ccfcf186ac190ccb8f91676958e6030b4224bfd46b3598261736fbfcb7c351f.svg)\n\n- the horizontal asymptote at ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) remains the same, and\n\n- the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept moves from ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to ![](images/e8/e8c4c8598a8228afb25cbf13c034e2fe234393f9b9c0dfde8be1726b4fc79a8e.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111833 #edb/ref 1065 1858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111833 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "78c17e05-8ef3-58fd-a9ea-a2fc04f4bd83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111833 #edb/ref 1067 "Vertical Compression" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507608 #edb/ref 1080 "images/a8/a853afe945aec222c4d12f75fbb8b322706791fb44fd7e716eb4385cd44edbeb.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507608 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507608 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "06874964-3a0d-5dab-8f71-b70c5a63dd33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507619 #edb/ref 1080 "2.5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507619 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507619 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0fb64125-a7d3-5b49-a2f6-4235214e697f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507637 #edb/ref 1080 "images/01/016f88de4ffd29f3b5721c767bf9e2db493ef7ac91dc2613ee7f3169dcba1718.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507637 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507637 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "21da3bba-8fd7-5e30-9f51-a68ed25d5f4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507639 #edb/ref 1080 "images/92/929a671186aad590fe4f771d9facb822b40fe43b77579720edd38fb8f3d22f25.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507639 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507639 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "236bd1ef-36c0-5ab3-a3a7-481538927b09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507656 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1