05 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508024 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508024 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508024 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186507658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508024 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d7f74135-1719-59e7-a4de-fc90530e85bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508025 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508025 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508025 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508025 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508025 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508025 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508025 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508025 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186507639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508025 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e13f8818-a437-5c98-9880-94f58b0fdfb0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508046 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508046 #edb/ref 1069 "q-28925" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508046 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508046 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following plots shows the graph of $y={6}^{x}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508046 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0f9540be-2088-5958-b35e-45b30741404e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508046 #edb/ref 1074 "The graph of $y={6}^{x}$ has a $y$-intercept of $(0,1),$ grows rapidly for $x>0,$ and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x<0.$ \n\nWhen $x=1,$ we have $y={6}^{1}=6.$ Therefore, another point on the graph is $(1,6).$ \n\nWe draw the graph through these points as follows:\n\n![](images/01/016f88de4ffd29f3b5721c767bf9e2db493ef7ac91dc2613ee7f3169dcba1718.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508053 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508053 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47739" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508053 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508053 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={5}^{x}?$\n\nI. Its range is $(-\\infty ,5).$\n\nII. $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to -\\,\\infty .$\n\nIII. It's an increasing function." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508053 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "302012e2-b0bf-56ed-8145-9c936fb36ce2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508053 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={5}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/31/31c990964a5a04c4a44773bb00d1b097999951b823e3b10861d13e38c364b8b5.png)\n\nNow, let's look at each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, since the range of $f(x)={5}^{x}$ is $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true, since for $x<0$, the graph gets close to zero as we decrease $x.$\n\n- Statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements II and III are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508075 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508075 #edb/ref 1069 "q-7235" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508075 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508075 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/90/90626ef73fbd9c0bd94802275961ca0c604a76f0d01faa1889b7dd3524ad6132.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({4}^{x}).$ What is the value of the constant $k?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508075 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7f281af5-b28c-5775-bf2f-eca0f2d16ebb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508075 #edb/ref 1074 "To plot the graph of $y=k({4}^{x})$ we take the graph of $y={4}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k)$\n\nComparing the point $(0,3)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508080 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508080 #edb/ref 1069 "q-46059" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508080 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508080 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}?$\n\nI. It's an increasing function\n\nII. Its domain is $x>0$\n\nIII. $f(x)\\to +\\,\\infty $ as $x\\to +\\,\\infty $" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508080 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8c063995-cffa-55cf-8462-64cb05fc0c3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508080 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/45/4531a531fb98f092751cec4552d6ead2bf828759842214f030618dba1ea85893.png)\n\n \n\nNow, let's look at each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n- Statement II is false, since the domain of the function $f(x)={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is true. For $x>0,$ the graph increases without a bound as $x$ increases to infinity.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508087 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508087 #edb/ref 1069 "q-7118" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508087 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508087 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/af/afeee9a0b7dd8a68368444ca475a6ed9493f0dfb007f4e8406b8fe2a32abf9e4.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({2}^{x}).$ What is the value of the constant $k?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508087 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d727472-a16c-5704-a3dd-e6b93f9d685f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508087 #edb/ref 1074 "To plot the graph of $y=k({2}^{x})$ we take the graph of $y={2}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k)$\n\nComparing the point $(0,2)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508090 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508090 #edb/ref 1069 "q-29360" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508090 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508090 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following plots shows the graph of $f(x)={(\\frac{5}{4})}^{x}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508090 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a5402e2e-2a63-5f18-9868-604f33f81924" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508090 #edb/ref 1074 "The graph of $y={(\\frac{5}{4})}^{x}$ has a $y$-intercept of $(0,1),$ grows rapidly for $x>0,$ and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x<0.$ \n\nWhen $x=1,$ we have $y={(\\frac{5}{4})}^{1}=\\frac{5}{4}.$ Therefore, another point on the graph is $(1,\\frac{5}{4}).$ \n\nWe draw the graph through these points as follows:\n\n![](images/92/929a671186aad590fe4f771d9facb822b40fe43b77579720edd38fb8f3d22f25.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
